title: An empty body returns undef before the raw and ndjson branches
updated: 2026-08-27T06:35:51Z
---

_request short-circuits on an empty body:

    if ($status_code == 204 || !defined($body) || $body eq '') {
      return undef;
    }

    return $body if $opts{raw};
    if ($opts{ndjson}) { ... return $events }

So the early return wins over both option branches, and an endpoint that
legitimately answers with zero bytes breaks its own documented return shape:

  * `raw => 1` promises the response bytes verbatim, but a zero-byte body comes
    back as undef rather than ''. A caller doing `length $tar` on the result
    warns instead of getting 0.
  * `ndjson => 1` promises an ArrayRef of events even for a one-object stream
    (karr #3), but an empty stream comes back as undef rather than [].

Reachable today through images->load (karr #20). Docker's `quiet` on
POST /images/load suppresses progress detail, and on an engine that suppresses
the stream entirely the body is empty, so load returns undef where its POD
promises an ArrayRef. Podman does not expose it -- measured 5.4.2 (API 1.41),
quiet unset, 0 and 1 all return the identical single
{"stream":"Loaded image: ..."} object, so `quiet` is ignored there outright --
but the transport should not depend on that.

The load POD documents the undef case rather than papering over it. The fix is
to move the empty-body check below the two option branches, so 204 and a
genuinely absent body still return undef while `raw` returns '' and `ndjson`
returns []. That changes a return value, so it needs its own regression test in
t/streaming_shape.t and a Changes line.
